      <div class="tg-note">
        <span class="tg-note__label">What This Part Is For</span>
        Two mistakes are made at the start of nearly every assurance argument. The
        first is to assume the Old Testament taught salvation by works and the New
        Testament corrected it — which makes the OT irrelevant to the question and
        also makes God's method change halfway through his own book. The second is
        to assume the tension between comfort and warning is a problem created by
        systematic theologians. It is not. It is in Ezekiel. It is in Deuteronomy.
        It is in the single verse of Habakkuk that the New Testament quotes three
        times. Before we can weigh the competing verses in Part II, we need to see
        that the competition is native to Scripture and that Scripture holds it
        without embarrassment.
      </div>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 1: WHAT "SAVED" MEANS
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="question">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">What "Saved" Means in the Old Testament</h2>

        <p>The Hebrew verb behind most of our "save" language is
        <em>yasha'</em> (יָשַׁע) — to bring into a wide place, to rescue from a
        constriction. Its noun forms give us <em>yeshu'ah</em>, salvation, and the
        name <em>Yeshua</em>. The picture is not primarily courtroom acquittal; it
        is a person hemmed in and then given room.</p>

        <p>In the Old Testament that rescue is overwhelmingly concrete: from Egypt,
        from enemies, from famine, from the pit, from death. This has led some
        readers to conclude that the Old Testament knows nothing of what we mean by
        salvation. That conclusion moves too fast. The concrete rescues are told in
        such a way that they interpret one another and build toward something
        larger, and the Old Testament itself says so — the psalmist who cries
        "restore to me the joy of your salvation"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Psalm 51:12">Psalm 51:12</a>) is not asking to be
        pulled out of a well.</p>

        <div class="tg-definition">
          <span class="tg-definition__term">yasha' / yeshu'ah</span>
          <span class="tg-definition__original">יָשַׁע · יְשׁוּעָה</span>
          <span class="tg-definition__def">To be delivered out of a narrow, pressing
          place into open ground; the deliverance itself. Used of military rescue,
          of legal vindication for the poor, of healing, of restoration from exile,
          and — increasingly through the Psalms and Isaiah — of a rescue from sin
          and death that only God can accomplish and that has not yet fully
          arrived. The word's trajectory across the canon is one of the clearer
          arguments that the Testaments are telling one story.</span>
        </div>

        <p>Alongside it sits the question this study is actually about: not only
        <em>are these people rescued?</em> but <em>are these people God's?</em> The
        Old Testament's word for that relationship is covenant, and covenant is where
        both the comfort and the warning live.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 2: EDEN
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="eden">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Eden: God Moves First</h2>

        <p>The first thing that happens after the first sin is that God comes
        walking and calls out, <em>"Where are you?"</em>
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Genesis 3:9">Genesis 3:9</a>). The question is not
        for God's information. It is the opening of a search.</p>

        <p>Three things are established in Genesis 3 that never afterward change.</p>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">One</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">The initiative is God's</div>
          <p>Adam and Eve hide; God seeks. Nothing in the narrative suggests they
          were moving toward him. The pattern of the whole canon — a God who comes
          looking for people who are going the other way — is set in the first three
          chapters and is never reversed.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Two</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">The remedy is provided, not achieved</div>
          <p>They sew fig leaves; the covering does not hold. God makes them
          garments of skin (<a class="ref" data-ref="Genesis 3:21">Genesis 3:21</a>).
          Christian readers have long heard a first sacrifice in that verse, and the
          text does not say so explicitly — but it does say plainly that the covering
          humans made for themselves was replaced by a covering God made for them.
          That much is on the page.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Three</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">Consequence is not cancelled</div>
          <p>They are clothed and they are exiled. The garden is shut. Mercy and
          judgment arrive in the same paragraph and neither one softens the other.
          Anyone who wants a Bible in which grace means the removal of consequences
          has to get past Genesis 3 first, and no one has.</p>
        </div>

        <p>This third point matters more for our question than it may seem. A very
        large amount of assurance anxiety comes from an unstated assumption that if
        God's people can suffer real loss, they must not really be God's. Genesis
        answers that before the question is asked.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 3: ABRAHAM
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="abraham">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Abraham: The Verse the Whole Argument Runs Through</h2>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          And he believed the LORD, and he counted it to him as righteousness.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Genesis 15:6">Genesis 15:6</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>Everything in the later debate is compressed into this sentence, which is
        why both Paul and James reach for it and why they appear to pull it in
        opposite directions.</p>

        <p>The verb translated "believed" is <em>he'emin</em> — the causative of
        <em>'aman</em> (אמן), the root behind <em>amen</em>. Its core sense is
        firmness, reliability, being solidly supported. To "believe" God in Hebrew is
        to rest one's weight on him. The verb translated "counted" is
        <em>hashav</em>, a bookkeeping word: to reckon something to an account.</p>

        <div class="tg-definition">
          <span class="tg-definition__term">'emunah</span>
          <span class="tg-definition__original">אֱמוּנָה</span>
          <span class="tg-definition__def">The noun from the same root. English has
          to choose between "faith" and "faithfulness"; Hebrew does not, because it
          does not regard them as two things. <em>'Emunah</em> is steadiness — the
          quality of a thing that holds when leaned on. It is used of Moses' hands
          held up until sunset (<a class="ref" data-ref="Exodus 17:12">Exodus
          17:12</a>) and of God's own reliability
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Deuteronomy 32:4">Deuteronomy 32:4</a>). This
          single fact about Hebrew vocabulary does more to dissolve the
          faith-versus-works impasse than most of the arguments built on top of
          it.</span>
        </div>

        <p>Note what Genesis 15:6 does <em>not</em> say. It does not say Abraham was
        righteous. It says righteousness was <em>credited</em> to him, on the basis
        of trust, in a context where he had just been told to count stars he could
        not count and promised a son he did not have. Nothing had been performed.
        The ledger entry precedes the obedience.</p>

        <p>Paul builds on that sequence with two observations the text supports
        exactly. First, the crediting happens in Genesis 15; circumcision is not
        commanded until Genesis 17 — so Abraham was reckoned righteous while still
        uncircumcised (<a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 4:9-12">Romans 4:9–12</a>).
        Second, the law comes 430 years later, and a later contract cannot annul an
        earlier promise (<a class="ref" data-ref="Galatians 3:17">Galatians
        3:17</a>). These are not creative readings; they are chronological facts
        about the Genesis narrative.</p>

        <p>James builds on the same verse from the other end
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="James 2:21-24">James 2:21–24</a>), pointing out
        that Genesis 15:6 is quoted again at the binding of Isaac — the point at
        which Abraham's trust was demonstrated under maximum pressure — and
        concluding that the faith which was credited was the kind of faith that
        eventually walked up a mountain with a knife.</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The Two Apostles Are Answering Two Questions</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">Paul asks: <em>on what basis is a person
          accepted?</em> James asks: <em>how do you tell whether the thing a person
          calls faith is faith?</em></p>
          <p>Both answer from Abraham because Abraham's life contains both moments —
          the crediting in chapter 15 and the proof in chapter 22, separated by
          decades and by a great deal of failure in between. Paul's opponent is the
          man who thinks he must earn standing. James' opponent is the man who says
          he has faith and has never once acted like it. Neither is arguing with the
          other, and the tradition has spent a great deal of energy inventing a
          quarrel that the texts themselves do not have.</p>
        </div>

        <p>Hold on to one detail for Part IV: <strong>the decades in between.</strong>
        Between the crediting and the demonstration, Abraham lies about his wife
        twice, tries to secure the promise through Hagar, and laughs at God. The man
        the New Testament calls the father of the faithful had a track record that
        would fail most modern assurance checklists. That is not a marginal
        observation; it is data about what "faith that works" actually looks like
        over a life.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 4: EXODUS GRAMMAR
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="exodus">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Exodus Grammar — Rescue Before Law</h2>

        <p>The exodus is the Old Testament's master picture of salvation, and its
        structure is unmistakable once you look for it. Israel is not rescued because
        they are obedient. They are not even especially willing — they complain
        before the sea and after it. They are rescued because God heard, remembered
        his covenant with Abraham, and acted
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Exodus 2:24">Exodus 2:24</a>).</p>

        <p>The law is then given to a people <em>already</em> redeemed, and the Ten
        Commandments say so in their opening line before a single command is
        issued:</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          I am the LORD your God, who brought you out of the land of Egypt, out of
          the house of slavery.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Exodus 20:2">Exodus 20:2</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>The sentence is the grammar of the whole Bible in miniature. The
        indicative comes first — <em>this is who I am and what I have done for
        you</em> — and the imperatives follow from it. Obedience is not the price of
        rescue; it is the shape of a rescued life. Every New Testament epistle is
        built the same way, which is why Romans 1–11 precedes Romans 12 and Ephesians
        1–3 precedes Ephesians 4.</p>

        <div class="tg-timeline">
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">Ex 2:24</span><span>God <strong>remembers the covenant</strong> — the cause of the rescue is God's prior promise, not Israel's condition</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">Ex 12</span><span><strong>Blood applied</strong> — the household is spared not by its merit but by a mark it did not invent; the lamb dies in the house's place</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">Ex 14</span><span><strong>"Stand still and see"</strong> — the sea is crossed by a people who contributed nothing but walking</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">Ex 20</span><span><strong>Law given</strong> — to people already out, already free, already his</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">Ex 32</span><span><strong>Golden calf</strong> — forty days later. The redeemed people apostatise almost immediately, and the covenant survives by intercession</span></div>
        </div>

        <p>That last row is the one usually skipped, and it is essential to this
        study. Within six weeks of the most spectacular deliverance in the Old
        Testament, the delivered people are worshipping a metal animal. The covenant
        is not maintained by their consistency. It is maintained because Moses stands
        in the breach and because God is "merciful and gracious, slow to anger"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Exodus 34:6">Exodus 34:6</a>) — a self-description
        given <em>in response to</em> the golden calf, not in spite of it.</p>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">Two Things Both True at Once</span>
          Exodus 32 records both that God does not destroy the nation <em>and</em>
          that three thousand men die that day. The chapter refuses to be used for
          either "sin does not matter to a redeemed people" or "sin ends the
          relationship." It is the earliest full statement of the tension this whole
          study is about, and it comes with no resolution attached — only a mediator
          standing between.
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 5: SINAI AND "IF"
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="sinai">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Sinai and the Word <em>If</em></h2>

        <p>Having established that rescue precedes law, honesty requires the other
        half. The Sinai covenant is drenched in conditions.</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          Now therefore, <strong>if</strong> you will indeed obey my voice and keep
          my covenant, you shall be my treasured possession among all peoples.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Exodus 19:5">Exodus 19:5</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p><a class="ref" data-ref="Deuteronomy 28">Deuteronomy 28</a> spends
        fourteen verses on blessings for obedience and fifty-four on curses for
        disobedience — a ratio that is itself a sermon. Deuteronomy 30 puts the
        choice in the plainest possible terms: <em>"I have set before you life and
        death, blessing and curse. Therefore choose life"</em>
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Deuteronomy 30:19">Deuteronomy 30:19</a>).</p>

        <p>Any account of assurance that cannot accommodate this material is not
        accounting for the Bible. God's covenant people were told, repeatedly, at
        length, with specifics, that continuing in the covenant's blessings depended
        on their covenant loyalty — and the rest of the Old Testament is the record
        of those curses arriving.</p>

        <p>And yet. Inside the same chapter of Deuteronomy that issues the choice,
        this is said:</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          And the LORD your God will circumcise your heart and the heart of your
          offspring, so that you will love the LORD your God with all your heart and
          with all your soul, that you may live.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Deuteronomy 30:6">Deuteronomy 30:6</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>Read the sequence of Deuteronomy 30 as it stands. Verses 1–5 assume the
        curses have already fallen and the people are scattered. Verse 6 promises
        that God will perform surgery on the organ of loyalty itself. Verses 11–14
        insist the commandment is not too hard or too far away. Verses 15–20 then
        set the choice.</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The Condition and the Enabling Are in the Same Speech</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">Deuteronomy does not choose between "you
          must love God" and "God will make you love God." It says both, in order,
          without apology.</p>
          <p>This is the single most important structural fact in the Old Testament
          for our question, and it recurs at every level of the canon. The command
          is real and the failure to keep it is fatal; the ability to keep it is a
          gift God promises to give. Systems that flatten this into one clause — in
          either direction — are tidier than Deuteronomy and less true to it.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 6: WILDERNESS
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="wilderness">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Wilderness Generation — Scripture's Own Warning Passage</h2>

        <p>Of all the Old Testament material bearing on assurance, one episode is
        singled out by the New Testament more than any other: the generation that
        came out of Egypt and did not enter Canaan.</p>

        <p>Their case is the hard one on purpose. They were not outsiders. They came
        through the sea, ate the manna, drank from the rock, saw the fire on the
        mountain. Whatever "belonging to the covenant people" means, they had it — and
        of the adults who left Egypt, only Caleb and Joshua crossed the Jordan
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Numbers 14:29-30">Numbers 14:29–30</a>).</p>

        <p>Paul reads the episode as directly applicable to the church, and the way
        he sets it up is striking:</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          Our fathers were all under the cloud, and all passed through the sea, and
          all were baptized into Moses in the cloud and in the sea, and all ate the
          same spiritual food, and all drank the same spiritual drink... Nevertheless,
          with most of them God was not pleased, for they were overthrown in the
          wilderness. Now these things took place as examples for us... Therefore let
          anyone who thinks that he stands take heed lest he fall.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 10:1-12">1 Corinthians 10:1–12</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>Paul stacks the word "all" five times to make the point that full
        participation in the covenant signs was not the issue. Then he says
        <em>these things happened as examples for us</em> — for the Corinthian
        church, who had also been baptised and also ate a covenant meal.</p>

        <p>Hebrews 3–4 works the same passage even harder, quoting Psalm 95 and
        drawing the conclusion that "they were unable to enter because of unbelief"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 3:19">Hebrews 3:19</a>), followed
        immediately by <em>"let us fear lest any of you should seem to have failed to
        reach it"</em> (<a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 4:1">Hebrews 4:1</a>).</p>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">Where Each Tradition Takes This</span>
          Everyone agrees the wilderness generation is a warning to the church; the
          disagreement is over what it proves. One reading: they show that
          covenant privileges without living faith save no one — the fall reveals
          what was never there. Another: they show that genuine covenant members can
          forfeit the inheritance. A third notes that the promise itself never
          failed — the nation still entered, in the next generation — so the passage
          concerns forfeiting a share in something that God nonetheless secures.
          Part III gives each of these its best hearing. For now, note only that the
          text is used by the New Testament as a live warning to believers, and that
          no responsible reading gets to make the warning hypothetical.
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 7: REMNANT
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="remnant">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Remnant — Israel Within Israel</h2>

        <p>From the prophets onward, the Old Testament develops a category that the
        assurance debate cannot do without: the <em>remnant</em> (<em>she'ar</em>,
        שְׁאָר). Not everyone inside the covenant community is, in the end, found to
        be among the people of God.</p>

        <ul>
          <li>Elijah is certain he is the only one left, and is told there are seven
          thousand who have not bowed to Baal
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Kings 19:18">1 Kings 19:18</a>). Note that
          Elijah could not see them — the true number was invisible to the most
          faithful prophet of his generation.</li>
          <li>Isaiah's son is named Shear-jashub, "a remnant shall return"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Isaiah 7:3">Isaiah 7:3</a>), and the felled tree
          of Israel is said to keep a holy seed in its stump
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Isaiah 6:13">Isaiah 6:13</a>).</li>
          <li>Micah, Zephaniah, Jeremiah, and Zechariah all speak of a preserved
          minority gathered after judgment.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>Paul takes the category over wholesale in
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 9:6">Romans 9:6</a> — <em>"not all who are
        descended from Israel belong to Israel"</em> — and in
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 11:5">Romans 11:5</a>, where the seven
        thousand become "a remnant, chosen by grace."</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The Visible and the Real</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">The distinction between the covenant
          community as it appears and the covenant people as God knows them is not a
          Reformation invention. It is Isaiah's.</p>
          <p>This matters for assurance in two directions at once, and both are
          uncomfortable. It means membership, baptism, attendance, and reputation are
          not the same thing as belonging — which unsettles the presumptuous. It
          also means the community's judgment about a person is not final either,
          in either direction — which should quiet the person who has been written
          off by a congregation, and check the congregation doing the writing off.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 8: CIRCUMCISED HEART
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="heart">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Circumcised Heart</h2>

        <p>The Old Testament's own critique of merely external covenant membership is
        older and sharper than the church usually credits. The image is savage on
        purpose: the covenant sign cut into flesh is demanded of the heart.</p>

        <table class="tg-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Text</th><th>Who acts</th><th>What is said</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Deuteronomy 10:16">Deut 10:16</a></td><td><strong>You</strong></td><td>"Circumcise therefore the foreskin of your heart, and be no longer stubborn"</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Deuteronomy 30:6">Deut 30:6</a></td><td><strong>God</strong></td><td>"The LORD your God will circumcise your heart... so that you will love him"</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Jeremiah 4:4">Jer 4:4</a></td><td><strong>You</strong></td><td>"Circumcise yourselves to the LORD; remove the foreskin of your hearts"</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Jeremiah 9:25-26">Jer 9:25–26</a></td><td>—</td><td>Judah is listed among the nations as "uncircumcised in heart," its physical sign counting for nothing</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Ezekiel 36:26">Ezek 36:26</a></td><td><strong>God</strong></td><td>"I will give you a new heart... I will remove the heart of stone"</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 2:28-29">Rom 2:28–29</a></td><td>—</td><td>"Circumcision is a matter of the heart, by the Spirit, not by the letter"</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>Read the "who acts" column down the page. The command and the promise
        alternate, and Scripture never explains how they fit together. Deuteronomy
        says <em>do this</em> in chapter 10 and <em>God will do this</em> in chapter
        30. Jeremiah issues the command; Ezekiel records the promise. The two are
        held together by placement rather than by argument.</p>

        <p>Paul's conclusion in Romans 2 — that the real Jew is one inwardly, and the
        real circumcision is of the heart by the Spirit — is not a new doctrine
        imported into the Old Testament. It is a summary of it. He is quoting
        Deuteronomy back to people who had it memorised.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 9: EZEKIEL'S TWO WORDS
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="ezekiel">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Ezekiel's Two Words</h2>

        <p>If you want the assurance tension in its sharpest Old Testament form, take
        it from a single prophet. Ezekiel says both of the following, and the church
        has never fully reconciled them.</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          But when a righteous person turns away from his righteousness and does
          injustice and does the same abominations that the wicked person does, shall
          he live? None of the righteous deeds that he has done shall be remembered.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Ezekiel 18:24">Ezekiel 18:24</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          And I will put my Spirit within you, and cause you to walk in my statutes
          and be careful to obey my rules.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Ezekiel 36:27">Ezekiel 36:27</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>Chapter 18 is the great chapter of individual responsibility, written
        against a proverb the exiles were using to blame their fathers. Its whole
        argument is that a person's own present standing before God is genuinely
        theirs, genuinely reversible in both directions, and genuinely their
        responsibility. Chapter 36 promises that God will act on the will itself so
        that obedience follows — and, notably, says he will do it "not for your sake"
        but for the sake of his own name
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Ezekiel 36:22">36:22</a>).</p>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">An Observation Worth Carrying Forward</span>
          Ezekiel 18 is addressed to people who were <em>using doctrine to avoid
          responsibility</em> — "the fathers have eaten sour grapes and the children's
          teeth are set on edge." Ezekiel 36 is addressed to people in despair, who
          had concluded that their bones were dried up and their hope was lost
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Ezekiel 37:11">37:11</a>). The same prophet gives
          opposite-sounding words to opposite conditions. That is not
          inconsistency; it is pastoral aim. Part IV builds a good deal on this
          observation, because the two errors are still the two errors, and the
          medicine for one is poison to the other.
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 10: HABAKKUK
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="habakkuk">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Habakkuk 2:4 — The Hinge</h2>

        <p>One Old Testament sentence is quoted three separate times in the New
        Testament, by two different authors, at the hinge of three different
        arguments. It is worth slowing down on.</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          Behold, his soul is puffed up; it is not upright within him, but the
          righteous shall live by his faith.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Habakkuk 2:4">Habakkuk 2:4</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>In context, Habakkuk has complained that God is using Babylon — a nation
        worse than Judah — to judge Judah, and has climbed his watchtower to wait for
        an answer. The answer is that the vision is for an appointed time and will
        not lie, that the proud will not endure, and that <em>the righteous will live
        by his 'emunah</em>. Given the setting, "faith" here carries its full Hebrew
        weight: the steady, holding-on trust of someone waiting for a promise that
        has not yet arrived. Habakkuk's righteous man is not making a decision; he is
        staying at his post.</p>

        <table class="tg-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Quotation</th><th>Argument it anchors</th><th>Emphasis</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 1:17">Romans 1:17</a></td><td>The gospel reveals a righteousness from God, received by faith</td><td><strong>Righteous</strong> — the basis of standing</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Galatians 3:11">Galatians 3:11</a></td><td>No one is justified by law, because the law is not "of faith"</td><td><strong>By faith</strong> — the mode of receiving</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 10:38">Hebrews 10:38</a></td><td>Do not throw away your confidence; you have need of endurance</td><td><strong>Shall live</strong> — the continuance</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>The Hebrews citation is the one usually left out of the sermon, and it is
        the most important for this study. Hebrews quotes the Greek version and
        <em>reverses the order of its two clauses</em>, so that the warning lands
        last:</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          But my righteous one shall live by faith, and if he shrinks back, my soul
          has no pleasure in him.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 10:38">Hebrews 10:38</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>Then the author immediately adds his own verdict: <em>"But we are not of
        those who shrink back and are destroyed, but of those who have faith and
        preserve their souls"</em>
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 10:39">10:39</a>) — and follows it with
        Hebrews 11, an entire chapter of people who kept going.</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The Verse That Refuses to Take Sides</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">The single Old Testament text that the New
          Testament chose as its motto for justification by faith is also a text
          about not shrinking back.</p>
          <p>Whichever party quotes Habakkuk 2:4 as a knockdown argument is quoting
          half of it. Paul and the author of Hebrews are not using two different
          verses; they are using the same verse, and between them they cover the
          whole ground — a righteousness that is received, and a life that is lived
          out of it, over time, under pressure, before the vision arrives. That is
          the through line of this entire study, and it was written down by a minor
          prophet on a watchtower.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 11: NEW COVENANT
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="newcovenant">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The New Covenant: God Undertakes the Human Side</h2>

        <p>The Old Testament ends without resolving its own problem. The law is good,
        the people cannot keep it, the curses have fallen, and the exile has happened.
        Into that failure the prophets speak of a covenant that will not work the way
        the old one did.</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          Behold, the days are coming, declares the LORD, when I will make a new
          covenant with the house of Israel... not like the covenant that I made with
          their fathers... my covenant that they broke, though I was their husband.
          But this is the covenant that I will make: I will put my law within them,
          and I will write it on their hearts... For I will forgive their iniquity,
          and I will remember their sin no more.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Jeremiah 31:31-34">Jeremiah 31:31–34</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>Four things are promised, and every one of them bears on assurance.</p>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">1</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">Internalisation — the law written on the heart</div>
          <p>The problem with the old covenant is not diagnosed as a defect in the
          law but as a defect in the people: "they broke it." The remedy is not a
          lower standard; it is a relocated one. The commandment moves from tablets
          to the heart, which means obedience becomes something a person wants rather
          than only something they are told.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">2</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">Universal knowledge — "they shall all know me"</div>
          <p>"No longer shall each one teach his neighbour... for they shall all know
          me, from the least of them to the greatest." Whatever else this means, it
          rules out a two-tier covenant people in which some members are inside the
          arrangement without knowing God at all. This is one of the strongest
          prophetic texts for the view that the new covenant community is, at its
          core, a regenerate one — and it is a genuinely difficult text for any view
          that treats new covenant membership as merely external.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">3</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">Forgiveness that is final — "I will remember their sin no more"</div>
          <p>Not "overlook," not "postpone." Hebrews seizes on exactly this clause
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 10:17-18">Hebrews 10:17–18</a>) and draws
          the conclusion that where there is such forgiveness, there is no longer any
          offering for sin — meaning the sacrificial system has been closed, not that
          the believer's sin has become insignificant.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">4</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">Divine causation — "I will cause you to walk"</div>
          <p>Ezekiel's parallel promise
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Ezekiel 36:26-27">Ezekiel 36:26–27</a>) is more
          explicit still: a new heart, a new spirit, the heart of stone removed, God's
          own Spirit placed within, and the result — <em>"and cause you to walk in my
          statutes."</em> The obedience that the old covenant commanded, the new
          covenant produces.</p>
        </div>

        <p>Jeremiah then attaches a guarantee that is easy to miss because it is
        phrased as astronomy:</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          If this fixed order departs from before me, declares the LORD, then shall
          the offspring of Israel cease from being a nation before me forever.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Jeremiah 31:36">Jeremiah 31:36</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>The stability of the covenant is pinned to the stability of the sun and
        moon — that is, to something entirely outside the covenant partner's
        performance. This is the Old Testament's own deepest ground of assurance, and
        it is worth noticing where it is placed: immediately after the promise of
        forgiveness, and immediately before a description of a rebuilt city. Comfort
        is grounded in God's fixity, not in the people's improvement.</p>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">The Honest Difficulty</span>
          If the new covenant includes "I will cause you to walk in my statutes" and
          "they shall all know me," why does the New Testament spend so much energy
          warning new covenant people not to fall away? That question is the engine
          of Part II and Part III, and no tradition answers it without cost. State it
          now and let it sit: <strong>the promises are unconditional in form, and the
          warnings addressed to the same people are unconditional in seriousness.</strong>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 12: THE THREAD
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="thread">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Thread, Stated</h2>

        <p>Pull the strands together and the shape of Old Testament salvation is
        remarkably stable across a thousand years of text.</p>

        <table class="tg-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Element</th><th>Old Testament</th><th>New Testament</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Who moves first</td><td>God seeks Adam; God remembers the covenant; God calls Abram out of Ur</td><td>"We love because he first loved us" (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 John 4:19">1 Jn 4:19</a>)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Basis of standing</td><td>Reckoned righteousness on the basis of trust (<a class="ref" data-ref="Genesis 15:6">Gen 15:6</a>)</td><td>Justified by faith apart from works of law (<a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 3:28">Rom 3:28</a>)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Means applied</td><td>Blood of the lamb on the doorpost; the whole sacrificial system</td><td>"Christ our Passover lamb has been sacrificed" (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 5:7">1 Cor 5:7</a>)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Order</td><td>Rescue, then law (<a class="ref" data-ref="Exodus 20:2">Ex 20:2</a>)</td><td>Indicative, then imperative (Rom 1–11, then 12)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>What is required</td><td><em>'Emunah</em> — trust that holds and therefore walks</td><td><em>Pistis</em> — "faith working through love" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Galatians 5:6">Gal 5:6</a>)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Real danger</td><td>Wilderness generation; Ezekiel 18; exile</td><td>Hebrews 3–4, 6, 10; 1 Cor 10; the seven churches</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Ground of comfort</td><td>God's name, oath, and the fixed order of sun and moon</td><td>Christ's finished work and intercession (<a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 7:25">Heb 7:25</a>)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Deepest promise</td><td>A heart God himself circumcises; his Spirit within</td><td>The Spirit as seal and down payment (<a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 1:13-14">Eph 1:13–14</a>)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>There is no point in that table where the method changes. There is no
        dispensation of works followed by a dispensation of grace. There is one God
        rescuing one kind of people in one way, and asking of them, from the
        beginning, one thing described by a Hebrew word that means both faith and
        faithfulness because it never occurred to Hebrew to separate them.</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The Through Line</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">God saves by promise, applies the rescue by
          blood, credits righteousness to trust, requires of the trusting a loyalty
          he himself undertakes to produce, warns them in earnest that abandoning the
          covenant is fatal, and stakes the outcome on his own name.</p>
          <p>Every one of those clauses is Old Testament before it is New. The New
          Testament does not add a clause; it supplies the name of the lamb, the
          identity of the mediator, and the arrival of the Spirit who does the
          causing. <strong>What is required of the saved person has never
          changed.</strong> It is to hold on to the God who has hold of you — and to
          keep walking while the vision tarries.</p>
        </div>

        <p>Part II takes up the New Testament texts that seem to divide against each
        other on precisely this point, and reads each one in its own context before
        any harmonising is attempted.</p>
      </section>

      <div class="tg-reading">
        <h2 class="tg-reading__title">For Further Study</h2>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>The Christ of the Covenants</cite>, O. Palmer Robertson. P&amp;R, 1980.
          <em>A standard covenant-theology treatment of the OT→NT continuity; clear on
          the structure of the covenants, and openly Reformed in its conclusions.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>Old Testament Theology</cite>, John Goldingay. IVP, 2003–2009.
          <em>Three volumes; unusually careful about letting OT texts say what they say
          before Christian systematics arrives. Useful precisely where it resists tidy
          synthesis.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>Covenant and Eschatology</cite> / <cite>God's Kingdom through God's Covenants</cite>,
          Peter Gentry and Stephen Wellum. Crossway, 2015 (abridged from
          <cite>Kingdom through Covenant</cite>). <em>Argues the "all shall know me"
          clause of Jeremiah 31 hard, in a direction that presses against
          infant-inclusive covenant models. The best statement of that case.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>The Epistle to the Hebrews</cite>, F. F. Bruce (NICNT, rev. 1990), on
          10:32–39. <em>The clearest short treatment of what Hebrews does with
          Habakkuk 2:4 and why the clause order matters.</em>
        </p>
      </div>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-part" id="part-2" aria-labelledby="part-2-h">
        <div class="tg-part__head">
          <span class="tg-part__kicker">Part II of Five</span>
          <h2 class="tg-part__title" id="part-2-h">The Competing Verses</h2>
          <p class="tg-part__intro">Two sets of New Testament texts appear to say opposite things about whether a
        believer can be lost. Most treatments quote one set and explain the other away.
        This part does neither. It lays both out in full, reads the six hardest
        passages closely in their own contexts, sets out the five standard ways of
        holding them together with the price each one pays — then shows that three of
        the five are one reading, and runs every hard text through it to demonstrate
        that none of them has to be forced.</p>
          <blockquote class="tg-part__verse"><p>Work out your own salvation with fear and trembling, <strong>for</strong> it
           is God who works in you, both to will and to work for his good pleasure.</p>
        <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Philippians 2:12-13">Philippians 2:12–13</a></cite></blockquote>
        </div>
<nav class="tg-toc tg-toc--sticky" aria-label="Part contents">
        <h2 class="tg-toc__title">Contents</h2>
        <ol class="tg-toc__list">
          <li><a href="#rules">Four Rules Before We Start</a></li>
          <li><a href="#keeping">The Keeping Texts</a></li>
          <li><a href="#warning">The Warning Texts</a></li>
          <li><a href="#hard">The Six Hardest Passages, Read Closely</a></li>
          <li><a href="#strategies">Five Ways of Holding Them Together</a></li>
          <li><a href="#pairs">The Verses That Need Each Other</a></li>
          <li><a href="#yield">What Honest Reading Yields</a></li>
        </ol>
      </nav>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 1: RULES
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="rules">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Four Rules Before We Start</h2>

        <p>The assurance debate is unusually prone to bad method, because both sets of
        texts are precious to the people quoting them and because the stakes feel
        personal. Four constraints keep the work honest.</p>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Rule 1</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">No verse gets to silence another verse</div>
          <p>"Interpret the unclear by the clear" is a good principle that is
          constantly abused, because each side declares its own favourites clear.
          John 10:28 is not more inspired than Hebrews 6:4. If a reading has to make
          one text mean approximately nothing, that is a cost, and the cost must be
          stated rather than hidden.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Rule 2</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">Ask who is being addressed and what for</div>
          <p>Almost every warning text is addressed to a congregation, in the second
          person plural, in a letter written to be read aloud. Almost every keeping
          text is a statement about what God does. These are different kinds of
          speech doing different jobs. A promise and a warning can both be fully true
          and fully addressed to the same room without contradiction — parents do this
          daily.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Rule 3</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">Distinguish the question being asked</div>
          <p>"Can God's grip fail?" and "Can I tell whether I am in his grip?" are not
          the same question, and answers to the first are constantly offered as
          answers to the second. Much of the apparent conflict dissolves — and the
          genuinely hard residue becomes visible — once these are kept apart.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Rule 4</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">Notice when a text is describing and when it is exhorting</div>
          <p>"He who began a good work in you will bring it to completion" describes.
          "Let anyone who thinks that he stands take heed lest he fall" exhorts.
          Turning an exhortation into a bare prediction, or a description into a
          condition, is the single most common move in this debate — and both sides
          make it.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 2: KEEPING TEXTS
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="keeping">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Keeping Texts</h2>

        <p>These are the passages a frightened believer should be able to find in the
        dark. They are not a minor strand; they run through the Gospels, Paul, Peter,
        John, Hebrews, and Jude.</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          All that the Father gives me will come to me, and whoever comes to me I will
          never cast out... And this is the will of him who sent me, that I should lose
          nothing of all that he has given me, but raise it up on the last day.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="John 6:37-39">John 6:37–39</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          My sheep hear my voice, and I know them, and they follow me. I give them
          eternal life, and they will never perish, and no one will snatch them out of
          my hand. My Father, who has given them to me, is greater than all, and no one
          is able to snatch them out of the Father's hand.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="John 10:27-29">John 10:27–29</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          For I am sure that neither death nor life, nor angels nor rulers, nor things
          present nor things to come, nor powers, nor height nor depth, nor anything
          else in all creation, will be able to separate us from the love of God in
          Christ Jesus our Lord.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 8:38-39">Romans 8:38–39</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <table class="tg-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Text</th><th>What it asserts</th><th>The force of it</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 8:29-30">Rom 8:29–30</a></td><td>Foreknew → predestined → called → justified → <em>glorified</em></td><td>The chain has no leakage; the last verb is past tense for a future event</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Philippians 1:6">Phil 1:6</a></td><td>"He who began a good work in you will bring it to completion"</td><td>The finisher is the one who started; the sentence has one subject</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 1:13-14">Eph 1:13–14</a></td><td>Sealed with the promised Holy Spirit, "the guarantee of our inheritance"</td><td><em>Arrabōn</em> — a down payment that legally commits to the rest</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 4:30">Eph 4:30</a></td><td>Sealed "for the day of redemption"</td><td>The seal's stated duration is until the end</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 1:8-9">1 Cor 1:8–9</a></td><td>He "will sustain you to the end, guiltless" — because "God is faithful"</td><td>Said to the <em>Corinthians</em>, of all churches</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="1 Peter 1:3-5">1 Pet 1:3–5</a></td><td>An inheritance "kept in heaven for you, who by God's power are being guarded through faith"</td><td>Guarded — a military word; note "<em>through faith</em>"</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="1 John 5:13">1 Jn 5:13</a></td><td>"That you may know that you have eternal life"</td><td>Assurance is stated as the letter's purpose</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 7:25">Heb 7:25</a></td><td>He is able to save "to the uttermost" because "he always lives to make intercession"</td><td>From <em>Hebrews</em> — the book of the warnings</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Jude 24">Jude 24</a></td><td>"Him who is able to keep you from stumbling"</td><td>Closes a letter otherwise entirely about apostates</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">Two Details Rarely Noticed</span>
          <strong>First</strong>, 1 Corinthians 1:8–9 promises that God will sustain
          the Corinthians blameless to the end — and Paul writes it to a church he is
          about to rebuke for incest, lawsuits, drunkenness at the Lord's table, and
          denying the resurrection. Whatever the promise means, it is not a reward for
          the congregation's spiritual health.
          <strong>Second</strong>, Hebrews 7:25 and Jude 24 are the strongest keeping
          statements in the New Testament, and they sit inside the two documents most
          concerned with falling away. The authors who warn hardest also comfort
          hardest. That is a fact about the canon that any reading has to explain.
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 3: WARNING TEXTS
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="warning">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Warning Texts</h2>

        <p>These are the passages usually handled quickly. They should not be. They
        are numerous, they are emphatic, they are spread across the same authors, and
        several of them are addressed to people the text describes in language
        otherwise reserved for believers.</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          Not everyone who says to me, "Lord, Lord," will enter the kingdom of heaven,
          but the one who does the will of my Father who is in heaven. On that day many
          will say to me, "Lord, Lord, did we not prophesy in your name...?" And then
          will I declare to them, "I never knew you; depart from me, you workers of
          lawlessness."
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 7:21-23">Matthew 7:21–23</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          If anyone does not abide in me he is thrown away like a branch and withers;
          and the branches are gathered, thrown into the fire, and burned.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="John 15:6">John 15:6</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <table class="tg-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Text</th><th>The warning</th><th>Why it is hard to defuse</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 24:12-13">Matt 24:12–13</a></td><td>"The love of many will grow cold. But the one who endures to the end will be saved"</td><td>Salvation is attached to endurance in the same breath as cooling love</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 11:20-22">Rom 11:20–22</a></td><td>"You stand fast through faith... they were broken off... provided you continue in his kindness. Otherwise you too will be cut off"</td><td>Written to Gentile Christians about their own standing, not about Israel's</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 9:27">1 Cor 9:27</a></td><td>"Lest after preaching to others I myself should be disqualified"</td><td>Paul applies it to <em>himself</em></td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 15:2">1 Cor 15:2</a></td><td>Saved "if you hold fast — unless you believed in vain"</td><td>Raises the category of a faith that was real enough to name and still vain</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Galatians 5:4">Gal 5:4</a></td><td>"You are severed from Christ... you have fallen away from grace"</td><td>The language of severance is as strong as language gets</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Colossians 1:22-23">Col 1:22–23</a></td><td>Presented holy and blameless "if indeed you continue in the faith, stable and steadfast"</td><td>A conditional attached directly to reconciliation's outcome</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="1 Timothy 1:19-20">1 Tim 1:19–20</a></td><td>Some "have made shipwreck of their faith" — named individuals</td><td>Concrete cases, not hypotheticals</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="1 Timothy 4:1">1 Tim 4:1</a></td><td>"Some will depart from the faith"</td><td>Predicted as certain, not merely possible</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 3:12-14">Heb 3:12–14</a></td><td>"Take care, brothers, lest there be in any of you an evil, unbelieving heart, leading you to fall away"</td><td>Addressed to "brothers"; "we share in Christ <em>if</em> we hold fast"</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 12:15-17">Heb 12:15–17</a></td><td>Esau, who "found no chance to repent, though he sought it with tears"</td><td>The possibility of a point of no return</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="2 Peter 2:20-22">2 Pet 2:20–22</a></td><td>"The last state has become worse for them than the first"</td><td>They had "escaped the defilements of the world through the knowledge of our Lord"</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 2:5">Rev 2:5</a></td><td>"Repent... if not, I will come and remove your lampstand"</td><td>Addressed to a church Christ is otherwise commending</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 3:5">Rev 3:5</a></td><td>"I will never blot his name out of the book of life"</td><td>A promise whose form presumes the alternative is intelligible</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The Shape of the Problem</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">The warnings are not rare, not marginal, and
          not confined to one author. They are one of the New Testament's standard
          modes of address to the church.</p>
          <p>Any account of assurance built only from the keeping texts will produce
          Christians who cannot hear a third of their own New Testament. Any account
          built only from the warnings will produce Christians who cannot rest. The
          canon evidently intends its people to live with both — which suggests that
          the two sets are doing complementary work rather than fighting.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 4: THE HARD SIX
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="hard">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Six Hardest Passages, Read Closely</h2>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">1. Hebrews 6:4–6</h3>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          For it is impossible, in the case of those who have once been enlightened,
          who have tasted the heavenly gift, and have shared in the Holy Spirit, and
          have tasted the goodness of the word of God and the powers of the age to
          come, and then have fallen away, to restore them again to repentance, since
          they are crucifying once again the Son of God to their own harm and holding
          him up to contempt.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 6:4-6">Hebrews 6:4–6</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>The descriptive terms are the battleground. <em>Enlightened</em>
        (φωτισθέντας) is used in <a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 10:32">Hebrews
        10:32</a> of the readers' conversion. <em>Tasted</em> (γευσαμένους) is used
        in <a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 2:9">2:9</a> of Christ tasting death — so
        the old argument that "tasting" means sampling without swallowing does not
        survive the book's own usage. <em>Shared in the Holy Spirit</em>
        (μετόχους) uses a word Hebrews applies to believers repeatedly
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 3:1">3:1</a>, "partakers of a heavenly
        calling"; <a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 3:14">3:14</a>, "partakers of
        Christ").</p>

        <p>The main readings, each in its strongest form:</p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>Genuine believers who genuinely apostatise.</strong> The plain
          sense of the vocabulary. Cost: it must then explain "impossible... to restore
          them again," which is a harder doctrine than most who take this line
          actually hold.</li>
          <li><strong>Covenant participants who were never regenerate.</strong> Every
          term describes real exposure to the Spirit's work in a community without
          requiring saving union — comparable to the wilderness generation, which is
          exactly the analogy Hebrews itself has just used in chapters 3–4. Cost: the
          vocabulary is the same the letter uses of its believing readers, and the
          author does not flag a distinction.</li>
          <li><strong>A real but hypothetical case</strong>, framed to shock. Support:
          the author says in the very next breath, <em>"we feel sure of better things
          in your case — things that belong to salvation"</em>
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 6:9">6:9</a>). Cost: a warning about an
          impossible danger is a strange thing to build a paragraph on.</li>
          <li><strong>Loss of maturity or reward, not salvation.</strong> Reads
          "restore to repentance" against the immediate context of moving on from
          elementary teaching (6:1–2). Cost: verse 8's "its end is to be burned" is
          not the language of a lost bonus.</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">The Detail That Constrains Everyone</span>
          Hebrews 6:9 immediately follows the warning: <em>"Though we speak in this
          way, yet in your case, beloved, we feel sure of better things."</em> And
          6:10 grounds that confidence in evidence — "God is not unjust so as to
          overlook your work and the love that you have shown for his name in serving
          the saints." The author warns severely and then reassures, and he reassures
          <em>by pointing at fruit</em>. That two-step is the pattern of the whole
          letter and it anticipates Part IV exactly.
        </div>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">2. Hebrews 10:26–31</h3>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          For if we go on sinning deliberately after receiving the knowledge of the
          truth, there no longer remains a sacrifice for sins... How much worse
          punishment do you think will be deserved by the one who has trampled
          underfoot the Son of God, and has profaned the blood of the covenant by
          which he was sanctified?
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 10:26-29">Hebrews 10:26–29</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>This is the harder of the two Hebrews warnings, because of five words:
        <em>"by which he was sanctified."</em> The person in view is said to have been
        sanctified by the blood of the covenant. Readings that treat the apostate as
        never having been in the covenant have to argue either that "he" refers to
        Christ (grammatically possible, since Hebrews elsewhere speaks of Christ being
        consecrated) or that "sanctified" here means set apart in the covenant
        community's external sense (defensible from Old Testament usage, and Hebrews
        is steeped in it) rather than inwardly renewed.</p>

        <p>Two contextual facts should be held alongside it. The sin described is not
        a lapse but a settled course — <em>"go on sinning deliberately"</em> is a
        present participle of continuing action — accompanied by trampling, profaning,
        and outraging. And the paragraph ends by addressing the readers as people who
        will not do this: <em>"we are not of those who shrink back"</em>
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 10:39">10:39</a>).</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">3. 2 Peter 2:20–22</h3>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          For if, after they have escaped the defilements of the world through the
          knowledge of our Lord and Saviour Jesus Christ, they are again entangled in
          them and overcome, the last state has become worse for them than the first...
          What the true proverb says has happened to them: "The dog returns to its own
          vomit, and the sow, after washing herself, returns to wallowing in the mire."
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="2 Peter 2:20-22">2 Peter 2:20–22</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>The description is of real reformation — they escaped, they knew the way of
        righteousness — and real reversal. But the proverb Peter chooses to close with
        does specific work, and it is easy to read past. A dog that returns to vomit
        and a washed pig that returns to mud have not changed species. The washing was
        external; the appetite was not touched. Peter could have chosen an image of a
        son leaving home. He chose an image about unchanged nature, and that choice is
        an interpretive datum whichever conclusion one reaches.</p>

        <p>Against that: the passage is aimed at false teachers who had been inside the
        community (2:1, "denying the Master who bought them"), and the "bought" language
        is itself contested in the same way.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">4. John 15:1–6</h3>

        <p>The vine passage is the most-quoted warning and the most linguistically
        debated. Two cruxes:</p>

        <div class="tg-definition">
          <span class="tg-definition__term">airei</span>
          <span class="tg-definition__original">αἴρει (John 15:2)</span>
          <span class="tg-definition__def">"Every branch in me that does not bear
          fruit, he <em>airei</em>." The verb standardly means "takes away, removes."
          A popular alternative renders it "lifts up" — the vinedresser raising a
          trailing branch out of the dirt so it can fruit — which is lexically
          possible (the word carries "lift" in e.g. John 8:59, taking up stones) and
          horticulturally real, but it is a minority reading and John's other uses lean
          toward removal. Honest summary: "takes away" is the more probable rendering;
          the alternative is not absurd and should not be mocked.</span>
        </div>

        <p>The second crux is "in me." The branches that are removed are described as
        <em>in me</em>, which is Johannine union language. Those who hold the
        never-truly-saved reading answer that the vine is a covenant-community image
        drawn from <a class="ref" data-ref="Isaiah 5">Isaiah 5</a> and
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Psalm 80">Psalm 80</a>, where Israel as a nation is
        the vine and fruitlessness within the nation is exactly the prophets' complaint
        — a strong argument, since Jesus is speaking to Jews at Passover, and Judas has
        just left the room in chapter 13.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">5. Galatians 5:4</h3>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          You are severed from Christ, you who would be justified by the law; you have
          fallen away from grace.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Galatians 5:4">Galatians 5:4</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>Note carefully what the falling is <em>from</em> and <em>into</em>. It is
        not a fall into immorality; it is a fall into law-keeping as the basis of
        acceptance. "Fallen from grace" in ordinary English means a moral collapse; in
        Galatians it means adopting a system of merit. The verse is therefore a
        remarkably poor proof text for the idea that sin costs a believer their
        salvation, and a very good one for the idea that abandoning grace as the
        principle of standing costs you the benefit of it. Both sides tend to misquote
        it.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">6. Matthew 7:21–23</h3>

        <p>The decisive word is <em>oudepote</em> — <strong>"I <em>never</em> knew
        you."</strong> Not "I knew you once." Whatever these people had — prophecy,
        exorcism, mighty works, and the confession "Lord, Lord" — it never at any point
        constituted being known by Christ.</p>

        <p>This is the single strongest text for the position that catastrophic
        end-state professors were never saved, and it is doubly striking because the
        works listed are not counterfeit religion but supernatural ministry. Note also
        what Jesus contrasts it with: "the one who <em>does the will</em> of my Father."
        The distinguishing mark is not the spectacular but the ordinary and obedient —
        which is where Part IV will land.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 5: STRATEGIES
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="strategies">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Five Ways of Holding Them Together</h2>

        <p>Each of these is held by serious readers of Scripture. Each explains a great
        deal. Each pays a price, and the price is named.</p>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Strategy A</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">The apostate was never regenerate</div>
          <p><strong>Anchor text:</strong> <em>"They went out from us, but they were
          not of us; for if they had been of us, they would have continued with us. But
          they went out, that it might become plain that they all are not of us"</em>
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 John 2:19">1 John 2:19</a>). Also Matthew 7:23's
          <em>never</em>.</p>
          <p><strong>Strength:</strong> John states the principle explicitly, and it is
          the only place in the New Testament where an apostate's departure is directly
          interpreted. That is a considerable advantage — it is the canon's own gloss.</p>
          <p><strong>Cost:</strong> it must give a thinner sense to "sanctified by the
          blood of the covenant" (Heb 10:29), to "shared in the Holy Spirit," and to the
          "in me" of John 15:2 than those phrases naturally bear. It also risks turning
          every warning into a warning about other people, which is the opposite of how
          the warnings are addressed.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Strategy B</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">Real believers can really fall away</div>
          <p><strong>Anchor texts:</strong> Hebrews 6 and 10 at face value; Romans
          11:20–22; 1 Timothy 1:19; 2 Peter 2:20.</p>
          <p><strong>Strength:</strong> it reads the warning passages as they read, in
          the persons they are addressed to, without special pleading. It takes
          seriously that the New Testament warns <em>Christians</em>, not observers.</p>
          <p><strong>Cost:</strong> the keeping texts have to be conditioned — "no one
          will snatch them" becomes compatible with walking out, which is a real
          strain on John 10:28–29, and "will bring it to completion" becomes a
          probability. It also has to say what "impossible to restore" means, and most
          who hold this view do not in fact believe restoration is impossible.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Strategy C</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">The warnings are the means of perseverance</div>
          <p><strong>The claim:</strong> God preserves his people <em>through</em>
          instruments, and the warnings are one of the instruments. The danger is real,
          the warning is real, and the warning works — which is precisely why the elect
          do not fall. Asking "but would they have fallen without it?" is like asking
          whether a person would have survived without the medicine they took.</p>
          <p><strong>Strength:</strong> it lets both sets of texts operate at full
          strength without reducing either, and it matches the observable rhetorical
          pattern — warn severely, then express confidence (Heb 6:9; 10:39). It also
          explains why 1 Peter 1:5 says "guarded... <em>through faith</em>": the
          guarding operates by sustaining the believing.</p>
          <p><strong>Cost:</strong> it can be accused of circularity, and it gives no
          direct help to the person asking "am I one of the preserved?" — it answers
          the metaphysics and leaves the pastoral question to be handled separately.
          Part IV is where that debt has to be paid.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Strategy D</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">What is lost is reward and inheritance, not salvation</div>
          <p><strong>Anchor texts:</strong>
          <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 3:15">1 Corinthians 3:15</a> — the
          work burns, "though he himself will be saved, but only as through fire";
          <a class="ref" data-ref="2 Timothy 2:12-13">2 Timothy 2:12–13</a>; the
          disqualification language of 1 Corinthians 9:27, drawn from athletics rather
          than judgment.</p>
          <p><strong>Strength:</strong> 1 Corinthians 3 does describe exactly this
          outcome, in so many words, and the New Testament clearly does teach a
          judgment of believers' work distinct from the question of their acceptance.
          The category is not invented.</p>
          <p><strong>Cost:</strong> it cannot be stretched over the whole warning
          corpus without breaking. "Its end is to be burned" (Heb 6:8), "a fury of fire
          that will consume the adversaries" (Heb 10:27), and "thrown into the fire and
          burned" (John 15:6) are not descriptions of a diminished reward. Applied
          across the board, this strategy produces the pastoral disaster the study's
          Part IV names — assurance detached from any relation to a changed life.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Strategy E</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">Covenantal — real membership, real severance, and an election that holds</div>
          <p><strong>The claim:</strong> the New Testament speaks of belonging on two
          levels, as the Old Testament does. A person can be genuinely inside the
          covenant community, genuinely participating in its blessings, genuinely
          sanctified in the "set apart" sense — and be cut off. Meanwhile God's elect,
          known to him, are not lost. Romans 11's olive tree is the model: real
          branches really broken off, a root that stands.</p>
          <p><strong>Strength:</strong> it handles Hebrews 10:29 and John 15:2 without
          straining their vocabulary, because it lets "sanctified" and "in me" mean
          what they say at the covenantal level. It has deep Old Testament roots
          (Part I's remnant).</p>
          <p><strong>Cost:</strong> it can leave the individual unsure which level
          describes them, and critics fairly ask whether it has solved the problem or
          relabelled it.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">There Is a Place to Stand</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">Strategies A, C, and E are not three competing
          options. They are three parts of one reading, they were never in conflict,
          and together they carry every text in this part without forcing any of
          them.</p>
          <p>It is fashionable to end a survey like this by saying that every position
          has its hard verses and the reader must choose. That is not true here, and
          saying it is a failure of nerve rather than a display of humility. A, C, and
          E answer three <em>different</em> questions — <em>what does apostasy reveal?</em>,
          <em>how does God keep his people?</em>, <em>on how many levels does Scripture
          speak of belonging?</em> — and the answers do not compete. The next table
          runs the hardest texts through the combined frame so the claim can be checked
          rather than taken on trust.</p>
        </div>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Running the Hard Texts Through It</h3>

        <table class="tg-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Text</th><th>What makes it hard</th><th>How the frame reads it</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="John 10:28-29">Jn 10:28–29</a></td><td>Absolute; embarrassing for any loss view</td><td>Full strength, unconditioned. Nothing needs softening <strong>(A)</strong></td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 8:29-30">Rom 8:29–30</a></td><td>Chain with no leakage</td><td>Full strength <strong>(A)</strong></td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 7:23">Matt 7:23</a></td><td><em>Oudepote</em> — "I <em>never</em> knew you"</td><td>Read exactly as written: never, at any point <strong>(A)</strong></td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="1 John 2:19">1 Jn 2:19</a></td><td>—</td><td>The canon's own gloss on apostasy, taken as such <strong>(A)</strong></td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="2 Peter 2:22">2 Pet 2:22</a></td><td>Real escape, real reversal</td><td>Peter's own chosen image: dog and washed pig, species unchanged <strong>(A)</strong></td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 10:29">Heb 10:29</a></td><td>"Sanctified by the blood of the covenant"</td><td>Set apart at the covenant level — the sense Hebrews is steeped in throughout <strong>(E)</strong></td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="John 15:2">Jn 15:2</a></td><td>Removed branches are "in me"</td><td>The covenant vine of <a class="ref" data-ref="Isaiah 5">Isa 5</a> / <a class="ref" data-ref="Psalm 80">Ps 80</a>, spoken to Jews at Passover <strong>(E)</strong></td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 11:20-22">Rom 11:20–22</a></td><td>Real branches really broken off</td><td>Its own image, unmodified: branches cut, root stands <strong>(E)</strong></td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 6:4-6">Heb 6:4–6</a></td><td>Believers' vocabulary throughout</td><td>Real covenant participation, really forfeitable <strong>(E)</strong>; the warning is how the readers are kept, which is why 6:9 follows <strong>(C)</strong></td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 10:12-13">1 Cor 10:12–13</a></td><td>Warning and promise, adjacent</td><td>The warning <em>is</em> the mechanism of the promise <strong>(C)</strong></td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Philippians 2:12-13">Phil 2:12–13</a></td><td>Fear and trembling, and God at work</td><td>The <em>gar</em> joins them; effort runs on supplied power <strong>(C)</strong></td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 3:14">Heb 3:14</a></td><td>"We share in Christ <em>if</em> we hold fast"</td><td>Holding fast is both the evidence and the appointed means <strong>(C)</strong></td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Galatians 5:4">Gal 5:4</a></td><td>"Severed from Christ"</td><td>Falling <em>from grace as the principle of standing</em> into law — context, not system, settles it</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="2 Timothy 2:12-13">2 Tim 2:12–13</a></td><td>Denial met with denial; faithlessness met with faithfulness</td><td>Apostasy and failure are different things; Peter is the worked example of the second</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>Not one row required a text to mean less than it says. The two verses that
        gave the most trouble in §4 — Hebrews 10:29's "sanctified" and John 15's "in
        me" — are handled by the two-level covenant reading at their <em>full</em>
        strength, and it is worth noticing that this reading was not invented for the
        purpose: it is the remnant framework Part I found running from Isaiah through
        Romans 9.</p>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">What Remains Open — and What Does Not</span>
          Two kinds of difficulty are constantly confused, and separating them is the
          point of this whole part.
          <strong>Exegetical strain</strong> — where a system must make a text mean
          less than it says — is a real defect, and it is what sinks Strategy D when
          generalised and what Strategy B carries at John 10:28–29. On this measure the
          combined frame is not merely ahead; it is, as far as the table above goes,
          clean.<br><br>
          <strong>Metaphysical mystery</strong> — how God's sovereign keeping and the
          believer's real responsibility interlock — is a different thing entirely, and
          the frame does not dissolve it. But neither does Scripture.
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Deuteronomy 30:6">Deuteronomy 30</a> commands the
          circumcised heart and promises that God will perform it, in one speech, with
          no reconciliation offered. Ezekiel writes both chapter 18 and chapter 36.
          Paul writes Philippians 2:12 and 2:13 as one sentence. <strong>A residue that
          sits exactly where Scripture puts it is not a weakness in a reading — it is
          evidence that the reading has stopped in the right place.</strong> The systems
          that have no mystery left are the ones that got there by dropping half the
          canon.
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 6: PAIRS
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="pairs">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Verses That Need Each Other</h2>

        <p>Several of the sharpest texts in this debate were written as pairs, with the
        two halves joined by a conjunction — and the halves are routinely quoted
        separately by opposing parties. Reading them whole does more work than most
        harmonising schemes.</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          Therefore, my beloved... work out your own salvation with fear and trembling,
          <strong>for</strong> it is God who works in you, both to will and to work for
          his good pleasure.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Philippians 2:12-13">Philippians 2:12–13</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>The conjunction is <em>gar</em>, "because." The reason to work with fear and
        trembling is not that the outcome is precarious — it is that <em>God himself is
        at work in you</em>. The presence of God's operation is given as the ground of
        the strenuous effort, not as its alternative. Whichever way a reader leans, this
        sentence forbids setting divine work and human effort against each other. It is
        the closest thing the New Testament has to a formula for the whole question.</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          If we endure, we will also reign with him; if we deny him, he also will deny
          us; if we are faithless, he remains faithful — for he cannot deny himself.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="2 Timothy 2:12-13">2 Timothy 2:12–13</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>An early hymn, and a genuinely difficult one, because the third and fourth
        lines pull against each other on purpose. Denial is met with denial; faithlessness
        is met with faithfulness. The usual resolution distinguishes <em>apostasy</em>
        (a settled repudiation) from <em>faithlessness</em> (the failure of a person
        who still belongs) — which is defensible, and is roughly Peter's story: he
        denied Christ, and was not denied. But the couplet resists being made tidy, and
        the reason given for God's constancy is not the believer's condition at all:
        <em>he cannot deny himself.</em></p>

        <table class="tg-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Half usually quoted alone</th><th>The half attached to it</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>"He who began a good work in you will bring it to completion" (Phil 1:6)</td><td>Written to partners "in the gospel from the first day until now" (1:5) — Paul's confidence rests on visible evidence</td></tr>
            <tr><td>"You have been saved through faith... not a result of works" (Eph 2:8–9)</td><td>"...created in Christ Jesus <strong>for good works</strong>, which God prepared beforehand" (2:10)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>"No one is able to snatch them out of the Father's hand" (Jn 10:29)</td><td>"My sheep <strong>hear my voice</strong>... and they follow me" (10:27) — the promise is made to a described people</td></tr>
            <tr><td>"Reconciled in his body of flesh by his death, to present you holy" (Col 1:22)</td><td>"<strong>if indeed</strong> you continue in the faith, stable and steadfast" (1:23)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>"Make every effort to confirm your calling and election" (2 Pet 1:10)</td><td>"His divine power has <strong>granted to us all things</strong> that pertain to life and godliness" (1:3) — the effort runs on supplied power</td></tr>
            <tr><td>"Take heed lest he fall" (1 Cor 10:12)</td><td>"God is faithful, and he will not let you be tempted beyond your ability" (10:13) — the very next verse</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>That last pair deserves its own sentence. The New Testament's most famous
        warning against presumption is followed immediately, without a paragraph break,
        by its most famous promise about God's provision under pressure. Paul did not
        experience these as being in tension. He wrote them as one thought.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 7: WHAT HONEST READING YIELDS
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="yield">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">What Honest Reading Yields</h2>

        <p>Four conclusions can be drawn from the texts themselves, before any system
        is adopted. They are the conclusions this study will build on, and they are
        deliberately modest.</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">One</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">The security promised is real and it is
          located outside the believer.</p>
          <p>Not one keeping text grounds security in the believer's strength,
          sincerity, or performance. The grounds given are the Father's giving, the
          Son's intercession, the Spirit's sealing, and God's faithfulness to his own
          name. Whatever a reader concludes about apostasy, no honest reading of the
          New Testament produces a security that rests on the believer's grip.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">Two</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">The warnings are addressed to the church, in
          earnest, and are not decoration.</p>
          <p>They are in the second person, aimed at people the writers call brothers
          and beloved, and are accompanied by concrete examples of people who did fall.
          A pulpit that never issues them is not preaching the New Testament, whatever
          its doctrine of perseverance.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">Three</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">Continuance is the New Testament's normal
          evidence of genuineness — evidence, not payment.</p>
          <p>"If you continue," "if you hold fast," "the one who endures." In every
          case continuance functions as the mark by which the reality is known, in a
          canon that has already said the standing was credited by faith apart from
          works. Part I found the same structure in Genesis 15 and Genesis 22.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">Four</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">The people warned hardest are the people
          comforted hardest, by the same authors, often on the same page.</p>
          <p>Hebrews warns of an impossible restoration and promises salvation to the
          uttermost. Jude describes apostates for twenty-four verses and then blesses
          the one able to keep you from stumbling. Paul says take heed lest you fall
          and God is faithful in consecutive sentences. This is not incoherence; it is
          how the New Testament pastors people — and it is the strongest evidence that
          comfort and warning are meant to be held together rather than traded off.</p>
        </div>

        <p>Part III now sets out how the major traditions have systematised these
        texts, each in the form its own best theologians would recognise — including
        where each one's weakest link lies.</p>
      </section>

      <div class="tg-reading">
        <h2 class="tg-reading__title">For Further Study</h2>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>Four Views on Eternal Security</cite>, ed. J. Matthew Pinson.
          Zondervan, 2002. <em>Classical Calvinist, Moderate Calvinist, Reformed
          Arminian, and Wesleyan Arminian positions, each with responses. The most
          efficient way to hear four advocates argue the same texts.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>The Race Set Before Us: A Biblical Theology of Perseverance and
          Assurance</cite>, Thomas Schreiner and Ardel Caneday. IVP, 2001.
          <em>The fullest development of the "warnings as means of perseverance"
