      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 1: MAP
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="map">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">A Map Before the Detail</h2>

        <p>Two separate questions get tangled together, and traditions divide
        differently on each:</p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>The security question:</strong> can a person who is genuinely
          in Christ end up lost?</li>
          <li><strong>The assurance question:</strong> can a person know now that they
          are in Christ, and on what grounds?</li>
        </ul>

        <p>These are independent. It is entirely coherent to believe salvation cannot
        be lost and still be unsure whether you have it — that is the historic Puritan
        anxiety. It is equally coherent to believe it can be lost and be confident you
        currently possess it — that is Wesley.</p>

        <table class="tg-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Tradition</th><th>Can the saved be lost?</th><th>Ground of present assurance</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Reformed</strong></td><td>No — the elect persevere</td><td>Christ's work, plus the Spirit's witness, plus fruit as corroborating evidence</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Lutheran</strong></td><td>Yes — faith can be lost</td><td>The objective means of grace: baptism, absolution, the Supper — deliberately <em>not</em> introspection</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Wesleyan</strong></td><td>Yes — by wilful sin or unbelief; restoration possible</td><td>The direct witness of the Spirit, confirmed by the indirect witness of a changed life</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Reformed Arminian</strong></td><td>Yes — but only by defection from faith, and then irremediably</td><td>Present faith in Christ; continuing trust rather than performance</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Roman Catholic</strong></td><td>Yes — by mortal sin; restored through the sacrament of penance</td><td>Well-founded hope and moral certitude; infallible certainty ordinarily denied</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Orthodox</strong></td><td>Yes — but the framing is questioned</td><td>Participation in the life of Christ in the Church; a journey, not a verdict</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Free Grace</strong></td><td>No</td><td>The bare promise of Christ, believed — expressly not fruit or conduct</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>Notice already that Lutherans and Free Grace teachers sit at opposite ends
        of the security question and give closely related answers on assurance: look
        away from yourself, at the promise. And notice that Reformed and Wesleyan
        writers, who disagree flatly on security, both make a changed life part of the
        evidence. The lines do not run where people expect.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 2: REFORMED
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="reformed">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Reformed — Perseverance of the Saints</h2>

        <p><strong>The position.</strong> Those whom God has effectually called and
        sanctified can neither totally nor finally fall away from the state of grace,
        but shall certainly persevere to the end and be eternally saved. The ground is
        not anything in them: it is the unchangeable decree of election, the merit and
        intercession of Christ, the abiding of the Spirit, and the nature of the
        covenant of grace.</p>

        <p><strong>What it does not say</strong>, though it is constantly accused of
        saying it: that the saints cannot fall into grievous sin. The Westminster
        Confession says the opposite — believers may fall into grievous sins, continue
        in them for a time, incur God's displeasure, grieve the Spirit, wound their
        consciences, and bring temporal judgments on themselves. The Canons of Dort say
        the same at length. "Once saved, always saved" as a slogan for moral indifference
        is not the Reformed doctrine; it is what the Reformed confessions were written
        partly to exclude.</p>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">The Reformed Doctrine of Assurance Is Not What Most People Assume</span>
          Westminster devotes a whole separate chapter to assurance, and it is
          strikingly cautious: infallible assurance <em>"doth not so belong to the
          essence of faith, but that a true believer may wait long, and conflict with
          many difficulties, before he be partaker of it"</em> (WCF 18.3). A true
          believer may lack assurance for years. It may be shaken, diminished, and
          intermitted. The confession that most strongly denies that salvation can be
          lost also most strongly denies that the feeling of certainty is guaranteed —
          and it grounds assurance on three things together: the divine promises, the
          inward evidence of graces, and the Spirit's testimony (18.2).
        </div>

        <p><strong>Strongest ground:</strong> Romans 8:29–30's unbroken chain,
        John 6:39 ("that I should lose nothing"), John 10:28–29, Philippians 1:6,
        and 1 John 2:19 as the canon's own interpretation of apostasy.</p>

        <p><strong>Best-known statements:</strong> Canons of Dort, Fifth Head (1619);
        Westminster Confession chs. 17–18 (1646); John Owen; Charles Hodge; more
        recently the "warnings as means" development in Schreiner and Caneday.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 3: LUTHERAN
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="lutheran">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Lutheran — Assurance Outside Yourself</h2>

        <p><strong>The position.</strong> Lutherans hold both halves of what other
        traditions treat as a contradiction. Salvation is by grace alone through faith
        alone, entirely apart from works — and faith can be lost, driven out by
        wilful sin and unbelief. Lutheran confessions therefore do not teach the
        perseverance of the saints in the Reformed sense, and Lutherans are not
        Arminians either, since they deny that the will contributes anything to
        conversion.</p>

        <p><strong>The distinctive move is on assurance.</strong> Precisely because a
        person could in principle fall, Lutheran pastoral theology refuses to locate
        assurance in self-examination. Introspection is regarded as a trap: the more
        anxiously you inspect your own heart for evidence of grace, the less you will
        find, because the heart is exactly what the gospel says is unreliable.
        Assurance is placed instead in what God has objectively done and continues to
        do outside the believer — <em>extra nos</em>.</p>

        <div class="tg-application">
          <span class="tg-application__label">Where a Lutheran Pastor Sends the Doubting</span>
          <p><strong>To baptism.</strong> "You were baptised" is a fact in the world,
          not a mood. Luther reportedly wrote <em>baptizatus sum</em> — "I am baptised" —
          on his desk to fight despair. The promise attached to it does not fluctuate
          with the believer's confidence.</p>
          <p><strong>To absolution.</strong> The declaration of forgiveness spoken by
          another person, out loud, addressed to you by name. It is external for the
          same reason a diagnosis is external: you cannot talk yourself into or out of
          it.</p>
          <p><strong>To the Supper.</strong> "Given <em>for you</em>." The words are
          the point; the sacrament is a promise delivered to a body that can taste it.</p>
          <p>The pastoral logic is consistent and, whatever one concludes about the
          underlying dogmatics, unusually effective with the anxious. It gives a
          frightened person something to look at that is not themselves.</p>
        </div>

        <p><strong>Strongest ground:</strong> the warning texts at face value, held
        together with an unyielding <em>sola gratia</em>; Romans 6:3–4;
        1 Peter 3:21; the "for you" of the institution narratives.</p>

        <p><strong>Best-known statements:</strong> Augsburg Confession XII;
        Formula of Concord, Solid Declaration XI; Luther's Large Catechism on baptism.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 4: WESLEYAN
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="wesleyan">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Wesleyan — The Witness of the Spirit</h2>

        <p><strong>The position.</strong> Salvation is by grace through faith; the
        relationship is real and is genuinely relational, which means it can be
        abandoned. A believer may fall away through wilful, unrepented sin or through
        the shipwreck of faith — and may also be restored, which Wesley preached
        directly and often, notably in his sermon "A Call to Backsliders."</p>

        <p><strong>The distinctive contribution is the doctrine of assurance itself.</strong>
        Wesley's most original pastoral teaching is that God ordinarily gives his
        children a conscious, present witness of their adoption:</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          For you did not receive the spirit of slavery to fall back into fear, but you
          have received the Spirit of adoption as sons, by whom we cry, "Abba! Father!"
          The Spirit himself bears witness with our spirit that we are children of God.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 8:15-16">Romans 8:15–16</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>Wesley distinguished the <strong>direct witness</strong> — the Spirit's own
        inward testimony, prior to any reasoning — from the <strong>indirect
        witness</strong>, which is the believer's own consciousness of the fruit of the
        Spirit in their life. He insisted on both, in that order, and warned against
        each without the other: the direct witness alone is open to self-deception and
        enthusiasm; the indirect witness alone produces a religion of anxious
        inventory-taking.</p>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">A Correction Wesley Made Himself</span>
          Early Wesley taught that no one is a believer without this conscious witness —
          a position that devastated sincere people who lacked the experience. He
          later modified it, allowing that a person may be a child of God without the
          full assurance of it, and distinguishing the faith of a servant from the faith
          of a son. The revision is worth knowing: the most experientially confident
          tradition in this survey found it necessary to make room for believers who
          did not feel it.
        </div>

        <p><strong>Strongest ground:</strong> Romans 8:15–16; Hebrews 3:12–14;
        Hebrews 6 and 10 read plainly; the moral seriousness of the whole
        exhortation literature.</p>

        <p><strong>Best-known statements:</strong> Wesley's sermons "The Witness of the
        Spirit" I and II, "The Spirit of Bondage and of Adoption," "A Call to
        Backsliders"; the Methodist Articles of Religion.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 5: REFORMED ARMINIAN
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="classical">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Reformed Arminian — Apostasy by Unbelief Alone</h2>

        <p>This position is routinely collapsed into the Wesleyan one and is genuinely
        distinct. It traces itself to Arminius himself rather than to Wesley, holds a
        thoroughly penal-substitutionary and imputational account of the atonement, and
        differs from Wesleyanism on what actually causes a person to be lost.</p>

        <p><strong>The position.</strong> A believer's standing rests on the imputed
        righteousness of Christ received by faith, not on their obedience. Therefore
        <em>individual sins — even serious ones — do not sever the relationship</em>,
        because the relationship never rested on performance in the first place. What
        severs it is the abandonment of faith itself: defection, apostasy, the settled
        repudiation of Christ. And because Hebrews 6 says so, that apostasy is treated
        as final and irremediable.</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">Why This Position Deserves Its Own Section</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">It is the only view in this survey that takes
          <em>both</em> "impossible to restore them again to repentance" and "not by
          works" with complete literalness, and builds a system in which they are
          consistent.</p>
          <p>The result is pastorally distinctive. A Reformed Arminian pastor tells the
          believer struggling with sin that their sin does not endanger their standing
          — the same thing a Reformed pastor says, arrived at by a different route.
          And the person who fears they have committed apostasy is told that the very
          fear demonstrates they have not, since apostasy is a repudiation, not a
          worry.</p>
        </div>

        <p><strong>Strongest ground:</strong> Hebrews 6:4–6 taken whole, including the
        impossibility clause; the sharp distinction Galatians draws between falling
        from grace and falling into sin (Part II, §4.5).</p>

        <p><strong>Best-known statements:</strong> Arminius' <cite>Declaration of
        Sentiments</cite>; the Remonstrance of 1610, Article 5 (which conspicuously
        left the matter open, pending further study of Scripture); in the modern era
        F. Leroy Forlines, <cite>Classical Arminianism</cite>, and Robert Picirilli,
        <cite>Grace, Faith, Free Will</cite>.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 6: CATHOLIC
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="catholic">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Roman Catholic — Hope, Not Certainty</h2>

        <p><strong>The position.</strong> Justification is a real interior renewal, not
        merely a declared status; it is received in baptism, can be increased, and can
        be lost — not only by unbelief but by any mortal sin. It is restored through
        the sacrament of penance. Perseverance to the end is a gift that cannot be
        merited, and the Council of Trent explicitly denied that anyone can know with
        the certainty of faith, apart from special revelation, that they have obtained
        it.</p>

        <p><strong>What that denial does and does not mean.</strong> Trent was rejecting
        a specific claim — that assurance is of the essence of saving faith, such that
        doubting one's own salvation is itself unbelief. It was not teaching that
        Catholics must live in terror. Catholic theology affirms a firm hope grounded
        in God's promises and the sacraments, and a practical or moral certitude
        available to a person in a state of grace. The Catechism's own summary is
        characteristically restrained: we cannot judge ourselves infallibly, and must
        entrust ourselves to the judgment of God.</p>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">Where the Reformation Argument Actually Was</span>
          It is worth being precise, because this is one of the most misdescribed
          disagreements in Christian theology. Both sides affirmed that salvation is
          by grace, that grace is unmerited, and that no one is saved without faith.
          The dispute was over whether justification is a declaration about a person's
          standing (Protestant) or a transformation of their state (Catholic), and
          whether assurance is normal Christian experience or presumption. The 1999
          Joint Declaration on the Doctrine of Justification registered substantial
          agreement on the basic truth of justification by grace through faith, while
          leaving real differences in place — including this one. Anyone claiming the
          issue was simply settled, in either direction, is overstating.
        </div>

        <p><strong>Strongest ground:</strong> 1 Corinthians 4:4 ("I am not aware of
        anything against myself, but I am not thereby acquitted"); 1 Corinthians 9:27;
        Philippians 2:12's "fear and trembling"; Matthew 7:21's insistence that
        confession without doing the Father's will is worthless.</p>

        <p><strong>Best-known statements:</strong> Council of Trent, Session VI
        (1547), chs. 9, 12–13, 15 and the attached canons; <cite>Catechism of the
        Catholic Church</cite> §§1861, 2005.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 7: ORTHODOX
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="orthodox">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Orthodox — The Question Reframed</h2>

        <p>The Eastern churches do not have a confessional paragraph on eternal
        security, and the reason is instructive: they regard the Western framing of
        the question as already conceding too much.</p>

        <p><strong>The position.</strong> Salvation is not primarily a verdict to be
        secured but a life to be lived — union with God, <em>theosis</em>, worked out
        in cooperation (<em>synergeia</em>) with grace within the Church's sacramental
        life. Asking "am I saved?" as a settled question about one's past is, on this
        account, a category error, roughly like asking a person mid-marriage whether
        they are married enough. The Orthodox answer to "are you saved?" is often
        phrased: <em>I have been saved, I am being saved, and I hope to be saved.</em></p>

        <p><strong>On assurance:</strong> confidence is real but is located in God's
        mercy and in participation, not in a moment of decision or a completed
        transaction. Introspective certainty about one's own state is regarded with
        suspicion — the tradition's ascetic literature treats confident self-assessment
        as a spiritual danger (<em>prelest</em>, spiritual delusion) rather than a
        goal.</p>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">A Historical Note Worth Knowing</span>
          The one time an Orthodox patriarch published a frankly Calvinist confession —
          Cyril Lucaris, in 1629, including predestination and perseverance — the
          Eastern churches repudiated it, most fully at the Synod of Jerusalem in 1672.
          That synod is the closest thing Orthodoxy has to a formal answer on this
          question, and its answer was to reject unconditional election and eternal
          security while equally refusing the view that salvation is earned. The
          episode is useful evidence that the Eastern reticence is a considered
          position rather than an absence of one.
        </div>

        <p><strong>Strongest ground:</strong> the whole New Testament vocabulary of
        abiding, walking, running, and enduring; 2 Peter 1:4's "partakers of the divine
        nature"; the fact that Scripture speaks of salvation in past, present, and
        future tenses and no Western system comfortably accommodates all three.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 8: FREE GRACE
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="freegrace">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Free Grace — The Promise Alone</h2>

        <p><strong>The position.</strong> Eternal life is received the moment a person
        believes the promise of Christ, and is irrevocable. Assurance is of the essence
        of faith: if you believe Christ's promise, you thereby know you have eternal
        life, because knowing you have it is simply what believing the promise means.
        Repentance and submission to Christ's lordship are urged on believers as the
        path of discipleship and reward, but are not conditions of receiving eternal
        life. The warning passages are read as concerning loss of reward, fellowship,
        or temporal consequence (Part II, Strategy D).</p>

        <p><strong>The concern driving it is genuine and should not be dismissed:</strong>
        that adding conditions to faith — even the condition of subsequent fruit —
        turns the gospel into a performance test, and produces congregations of people
        who can never be sure because there is always more fruit that might have been
        borne. Anyone who has counselled a scrupulous believer knows this problem is
        real.</p>

        <p><strong>The counter-position within evangelicalism</strong> — usually called
        Lordship Salvation, and associated with John MacArthur's <cite>The Gospel
        According to Jesus</cite> (1988) — argues that saving faith by definition
        includes receiving Christ as he is, Lord as well as Saviour, and that a "faith"
        that changes nothing is the very thing James calls dead. The 1980s–90s exchange
        between these two camps is the sharpest intra-evangelical assurance debate of
        the modern era, and both sides landed real blows.</p>

        <p><strong>Strongest ground:</strong> John 5:24 ("has eternal life... has passed
        from death to life"); John 6:47; 1 John 5:13 as a statement of purpose;
        1 Corinthians 3:15's saved-through-fire.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 9: WEAKEST LINKS
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="weak">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Every System's Weakest Link</h2>

        <p>Stated as briefly and as evenly as they can be. Each is the objection that
        the tradition's own thoughtful members raise internally.</p>

        <table class="tg-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Tradition</th><th>Where it strains</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Reformed</strong></td>
              <td>It must read the warning passages as addressed to people who cannot
              actually be in the danger described, or as means to an end already
              guaranteed. And its own doctrine of assurance can generate the
              introspective spiral it was meant to cure: if apostates were never saved,
              then any evidence I might cite could equally belong to a temporary
              believer — which is precisely the Puritan "unpardonable anxiety" its own
              pastors spent centuries treating.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Lutheran</strong></td>
              <td>Holding "faith can be lost" together with "you contribute nothing to
              faith" leaves the mechanism of loss underdetermined. If God gives faith
              monergistically, why is its loss not equally in his hands? Lutherans
              generally answer that Scripture asserts both and declines to systematise,
              which is honest but is not an explanation.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Wesleyan</strong></td>
              <td>The threshold problem. If wilful sin can forfeit salvation, how wilful
              and how much? In practice the doctrine either becomes vague — and then
              does no work — or becomes precise, and then produces the scrupulosity that
              Wesley himself fought. The direct witness of the Spirit is also difficult
              to distinguish, in the moment, from a mood.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Reformed Arminian</strong></td>
              <td>The irremediability of apostasy is consistent but pastorally
              formidable, and it sits awkwardly beside the restorations Scripture
              actually narrates — Peter, David, the Corinthian offender, the prodigal.
              It must argue that none of these crossed the line, which may be right but
              cannot be verified from the outside.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Roman Catholic</strong></td>
              <td>The denial of ordinary assurance is hard to square with 1 John, whose
              stated purpose is "that you may <em>know</em> that you have eternal life,"
              and with the confident tone of Romans 8:38–39. The mortal/venial
              distinction also requires more precision about the state of a person's
              soul than most people can honestly manage about themselves.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Orthodox</strong></td>
              <td>Reframing the question is legitimate, but people still ask it, and
              the tradition offers them comparatively little that is direct. Its
              suspicion of assurance is a real safeguard against presumption and a real
              liability for the despairing.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Free Grace</strong></td>
              <td>It has to make Matthew 7:21–23, James 2, and 1 John's tests mean
              something other than what they appear to mean — and 1 John in particular
              is fatal territory, since it offers moral and relational tests <em>as</em>
              the ground of knowing. The pastoral risk is the mirror image of the
              Wesleyan one: assurance so detached from life that it can comfort someone
              whom the New Testament would not comfort.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The Assessment, Stated Plainly</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">These positions are not equally well supported,
          and this study does not pretend otherwise. The frame demonstrated at the end
          of Part II — 1 John 2:19 on what apostasy reveals, the warnings as God's real
          means of preserving real people, and the two-level covenant reading — carries
          every hard text without forcing one, and that is not a tie.</p>
          <p>Fairness in <em>representation</em> is not the same as refusing to
          <em>adjudicate</em>. Every position above has been stated as its own best
          advocates state it, which is a duty owed to them; having done that, the
          verdict is that this one reads the canon best, and it is the frame Parts IV
          and V build on.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">Three Qualifications That Are Not Retractions</span>
          <strong>First, the residue is real but it is the right residue.</strong> The
          frame does not explain how God's keeping and the believer's responsibility
          interlock. Neither does Deuteronomy 30, or Ezekiel, or Philippians 2:12–13.
          A reading that stops where Scripture stops has not failed.<br><br>
          <strong>Second, the Reformed pastoral weakness listed above is genuine and is
          not rhetorical balance.</strong> A doctrine of perseverance can and does
          produce introspective misery when it is preached without the Lutheran
          instinct to look outward and the Wesleyan expectation of the Spirit's
          witness. That is a defect in the preaching rather than in the position, but
          it is a defect that has wounded real people for four centuries, and it is
          why Part IV is written to hold all three instincts together.<br><br>
          <strong>Third, being wrong on the mechanism is not the same as being lost.</strong>
          The Wesleyan, the Lutheran, the Catholic, the Orthodox believer — these are
          brothers and sisters holding a different account of how they are kept, not a
          different Saviour. Read §11 below before drawing any other conclusion from
          this section: on what to actually <em>do</em> at two in the morning, all seven
          converge, and that convergence is itself evidence about where the real
          weight of this subject lies.
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 10: AGREEMENT
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="agree">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">What They All Agree On</h2>

        <p>This is the section most often left out, and it changes the temperature of
        the whole argument. Every tradition surveyed above affirms all of the
        following, without qualification.</p>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Agreed</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">No one is saved by their own works</div>
          <p>Including Trent, whose canons anathematise the idea that a person can be
          justified by works done without the grace of Christ, and which teaches that
          the very beginning of justification is unmerited prevenient grace. The
          caricature that Catholics believe in earning heaven is not what the Council
          taught.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Agreed</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">A settled, unrepentant life of rebellion is incompatible with salvation</div>
          <p>Reformed: it shows the faith was never real. Wesleyan: it forfeits.
          Catholic: it is mortal sin. Orthodox: it is the abandonment of the path. Free
          Grace, alone, would resist the inference — and even Free Grace teachers do not
          tell such a person they are fine; they tell them they are in grave danger of
          judgment as believers. <strong>Nobody in this survey offers comfort to the
          person living in deliberate, settled, unrepented sin.</strong> That is worth
          saying loudly, because the belief that some tradition somewhere licenses it
          is what makes people afraid of the doctrine of grace.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Agreed</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">A struggling believer who hates their sin is not in the danger the warnings describe</div>
          <p>Every tradition distinguishes the person fighting sin from the person who
          has made peace with it. The warnings target apostasy, hardening, contempt,
          and settled repudiation — not weakness, not relapse, not failure. This
          consensus is nearly total and is the single most useful thing in this part
          for anyone actually frightened.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Agreed</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">Anyone who wants to return may return</div>
          <p>No tradition teaches that a person who desires repentance should be turned
          away. Even the Reformed Arminian reading of Hebrews 6 holds that the desire
          for restoration is itself evidence that the irreversible line has not been
          crossed. The practical upshot is unanimous across seven traditions: <em>if you
          want to come back, the door is open, and your wanting to is evidence.</em></p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Agreed</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">Assurance is not the same as salvation</div>
          <p>Traditions differ on whether assurance is normal, but all distinguish
          having eternal life from feeling sure of it. This means the absence of a
          feeling is not proof of the absence of the thing — a point that belongs in
          more pulpits than it reaches.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 11: 2AM
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="2am">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Question at Two in the Morning</h2>

        <p>Systems are argued in daylight. The question arrives at night, and it is
        never phrased as a system. It is phrased: <em>after what I did, after how long
        it has been, after how cold I have gone — am I still his?</em></p>

        <p>Here is what is worth knowing before Part IV takes that question up
        properly. <strong>The traditions converge almost entirely on the counsel they
        give at that hour</strong>, and they converge on something none of their
        polemics would predict. Not one of them answers by telling the person to
        determine which system is correct. Every one of them, in its own idiom, says
        the same three things:</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The Convergent Counsel</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text"><strong>Look at Christ, not at yourself.
          Come now, not when you are better. And the anxiety you feel is not the mark
          of an apostate.</strong></p>
          <p>The Lutheran says: you were baptised. The Reformed says: he began it and
          he will finish it. The Wesleyan says: ask the Spirit and he will witness. The
          Catholic says: go to confession, and receive absolution. The Orthodox says:
          come to the chalice, and keep walking. The Reformed Arminian says: the fact
          that you are asking proves you have not repudiated him. They are not saying
          the same thing about the metaphysics. They are saying the same thing to the
          person.</p>
        </div>

        <p>Part IV takes that counsel and turns it into something usable — the actual
        tests Scripture gives, how to run them, and the very different medicine needed
        by the two very different people who read an article like this one.</p>
      </section>

      <div class="tg-reading">
        <h2 class="tg-reading__title">For Further Study</h2>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>Canons of Dort</cite>, Fifth Head of Doctrine, and
          <cite>Westminster Confession of Faith</cite>, chapters 17–18.
          <em>Short, primary, and routinely misquoted. Chapter 18 in particular is
          worth reading by anyone who thinks Reformed assurance is glib.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>Council of Trent, Session VI: Decree on Justification</cite> (1547).
          <em>The primary text. Read the chapters, not only the canons — the canons
          are the anathemas and read far harsher out of context.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          John Wesley, <cite>Sermons</cite> 10 and 11, "The Witness of the Spirit."
          <em>The foundational statement of experiential assurance, and more careful
          than its reputation.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>Grace, Faith, Free Will</cite>, Robert E. Picirilli. Randall House,
          2002. <em>The clearest modern statement of the Reformed Arminian position
          and its difference from Wesleyanism.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>The Orthodox Church</cite> and <cite>The Orthodox Way</cite>, Kallistos
          Ware. <em>The standard accessible entry to why the East declines the
          Western framing.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>The Gospel According to Jesus</cite>, John MacArthur (1988), and
          <cite>Absolutely Free!</cite>, Zane Hodges (1989). <em>The Lordship
          controversy from both sides. Read together or not at all.</em>
        </p>
      </div>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-part" id="part-4" aria-labelledby="part-4-h">
        <div class="tg-part__head">
          <span class="tg-part__kicker">Part IV of Five</span>
          <h2 class="tg-part__title" id="part-4-h">Testing Your Faith</h2>
          <p class="tg-part__intro">Scripture does not tell believers to wonder. It gives them tests, states
        plainly that the tests are meant to produce knowledge, and expects most people
        who run them honestly to pass. This part sets out the tests, shows how they
        are meant to be run — and how they are commonly run in a way that produces the
        opposite of what they were given for — and then separates the two readers who
        need opposite words.</p>
          <blockquote class="tg-part__verse"><p>I write these things to you who believe in the name of the Son of God, that
           you may know that you have eternal life.</p>
        <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="1 John 5:13">1 John 5:13</a></cite></blockquote>
        </div>
